# script-install.list — tools distributed ONLY as an upstream shell
# installer (`curl … | sh`), with NO package in brew/apt/pacman/dnf/snap/
# aur. This is the last-resort lane of the install policy (CLAUDE.md §6):
# a pinned remote installer, used only where nothing native exists.
#
# scripts/run-script-installers reads this file and runs each entry's
# installer ONLY on hosts where the tool isn't already available — it
# probes `command -v <bin>` first and skips anything already on PATH. So
# on an OS whose package manager DID provide the tool (e.g. macOS via
# `brew "herdr"`), the native lane installs it earlier in the run and this
# lane is a no-op. Net effect: the remote script fires only on "OSes
# without a package installer" for that tool.
#
# SECURITY: every line here executes remote code as your user. Pin the
# installer to the tool's documented command, review it before adding, and
# prefer installers that land in ~/.local/bin (already on PATH, user-
# scoped, easy to remove). This is a work machine (TISAX/ISO 27001) — treat
# additions to this file as you would any `curl | sh`.
#
# Format — one tool per line:
#   <probe-binary>   <installer shell command>
# Field 1 (up to the first whitespace) is the binary the runner probes with
# `command -v`. The rest of the line is the command, run verbatim via
# `sh -c`. Only FULL-LINE `#` comments are stripped — inline `#` is NOT
# supported here (installer commands may legitimately contain `#`).

# --- agent / terminal tooling ---
herdr   curl -fsSL https://herdr.dev/install.sh | sh
